import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The audio package: sound files from a handler (`Audio.play!`), and the plugin whose script is the
## player (`AudioPlayer.plugin`). The showcase's games play their sounds through it.
meta = {
	title: "Audio",
	description: "packages/audio plays sound files from a handler in a Roc Solid app: any file the browser can play, at a volume and a rate. It is a plugin, whose script is the player every page loads.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "play", label: "Playing a sound" },
		{ id: "when", label: "When a sound plays" },
		{ id: "player", label: "How the player works" },
		{ id: "files", label: "Sound files" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · audio"
				title="A sound from a handler, in one line"
			>
				<code>{"Audio.play!(\"/sounds/eat.wav\")"}</code> plays a sound when someone clicks, presses a key or
				wins a game: any file the browser can play (WAV, MP3, Ogg), from your site or any URL, with nothing
				to load in advance. The <a href={"${Paths.showcase}#sound"}>showcase's games</a> play their sounds
				this way.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				<code>Audio</code> is the module you call. Both halves of an app compile it, so a component that plays
				a sound type-checks on the server too. <code>AudioPlayer</code> is a
				<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem}#plugins"}>plugin</a> that serves the player, a small script every page
				loads. An app that plays no sound doesn't list it, and its pages load no player.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in your app's header, and list the plugin in your program:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.audio_header.file}, its header"} source={Sources.audio_header.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.audio_install.file}, in part"} source={Sources.audio_install.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="play">Playing a sound</Heading>
			<p>Import <code>audio.Audio</code> in any component, page or module, and call it from a handler:</p>
			<Code
				source={"import audio.Audio\n\n<button on:click={|| Audio.play!(\"/sounds/eat.wav\")}>Eat</button>\n<button on:click={|| Audio.play_with!(\"/sounds/pop.wav\", { volume: 0.5, rate: 2.0 })}>Pop</button>"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				<code>Audio.play!</code> plays a file at its own volume and speed. <code>Audio.play_with!</code> takes a
				<code>volume</code> from 0 to 1 and a <code>rate</code>: 2.0 plays twice as fast and an octave higher,
				so one short file can be a whole scale, as the showcase's flock plays its birds. A path starting with
				<code>/</code> is your site's; a whole URL plays from anywhere. The whole module:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.audio_module.file} source={Sources.audio_module.rocx} />

			<Heading id="when">When a sound plays</Heading>
			<p>
				A play is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#handlers"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a>, a call to the
				page's own JavaScript, and follows its rules:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					It runs once the handler is done and the page shows what it wrote. A handler that waits for the
					server runs twice, but plays once.
				</li>
				<li>
					Only a handler plays: an event's handler, a timer, or an effect's apply. Calling it while a hole, a
					memo or a component's body is computed is an error, and so is calling it on the server.
				</li>
				<li>
					Browsers keep a page silent until the reader has clicked or typed on it. Until then nothing plays,
					and no error is reported.
				</li>
				<li>
					A file that can't be played is reported on the browser's console. So is a play when the app
					doesn't list the plugin: "no effect is called audio.play: the audio plugin's script registers it:
					is the audio plugin in the app's plugins?"
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="player">How the player works</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin serves its script at <code>/rocx/plugins/audio/audio.js</code>. Every page loads it before
				<code>start.js</code>, so it is ready before the page wakes. It registers the browser effect
				<code>audio.play</code> on <code>window.rocxEffects</code>, where <code>Browser.call!</code> looks. Each
				play makes an HTML <code>Audio</code> element, sets its volume and rate (kept between 0 and 1, and
				between 1/16 and 16), and plays it. The browser fetches a file on its first play, and caches it.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>under</code>, which the runtime gives every effect, puts a path starting with <code>/</code>
				under the site's base: <code>/sounds/eat.wav</code> becomes <code>/rocx/sounds/eat.wav</code> when your
				site is served at <code>/rocx/</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.audio_player.file} source={Sources.audio_player.rocx} />
			<p>
				On the Android host no script is needed: the phone's own handler for <code>audio.play</code> plays
				the file, fetched from your app's server, at the same volume and rate. The
				<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>pomodoro example</a> plays its chime this way.
			</p>

			<Heading id="files">Sound files</Heading>
			<p>
				The package brings no sounds. Your app serves its own, like any static file
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}><code>--static</code></a>). This site's are a few KB
				each, chiptune notes made by <code>site/sounds.mjs</code>; the quiz and pomodoro examples make theirs
				the same way.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>A sound can't be stopped, paused or looped.</li>
				<li>A play answers nothing: your code can't tell when a sound ends, or whether it played.</li>
				<li>No loading ahead: the first play waits for the download.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The package's <code>README.md</code> (<code>packages/audio/</code>) has the full reference.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
